<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Keladi Chennamma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1696</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keladi">Keladi</a> Chennamma was queen of the Keladi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaka</a> kingdom in coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, ruling from 1671 until her death in 1696 after the death of her husband Somashekhara Nayaka. She defended the kingdom against neighbouring powers and, around 1689, gave refuge to Rajaram, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a>, as he fled the Mughal army towards Jinji, enduring a Mughal attack in consequence. She is remembered as one of the notable women rulers of Karnataka.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/keladi-chennamma.jpg" alt="Keladi Chennamma" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Balraj D · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ganapathi_%26_Keladi_Chennamma_Statue.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
